.SH NAME
veyon-worker \- Veyon Worker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B veyon-worker
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.\" TeX users may be more comfortable with the \fB<whatever>\fP and
.\" \fI<whatever>\fP escape sequences to invode bold face and italics,
.\" respectively.

\fBVEYON-WORKER\fR is  helper program started by the server to provide
specific functions in an isolated environment or in the context of the
user that is currently logged in. Those specific functions include the
demo server for the teacher computer and the demo client on the student
computers.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/veyon/
Log files of Veyon processes running as root, e.g. the Veyon Service
(\fIVeyonService.log\fR), Veyon Server instances running as root and the
authentication helper. The directory is owned by root with mode 0750 and is
created if missing. If it is not owned by root, is writable by group or others
or is a symbolic link, file logging is disabled and the reason is written to
the standard error output and the system log.
.TP
.I <log file directory>/veyon-<login name>/
Log files of Veyon processes running as an unprivileged user, e.g. Veyon
Master, Veyon Server and Veyon Worker instances in a user session. The log file
directory is configured with \fILogging/LogFileDirectory\fR (default:
\fI$TEMP\fR, i.e. \fI/tmp\fR). The per-user directory is owned by the user with
mode 0700 and is created if missing. If it is owned by another user or is a
symbolic link, file logging is disabled instead of falling back to a shared
location.

.SH SEE ALSO
veyon-server(1), veyon-master(1), veyon-configurator(1), veyon-auth-helper(8)
.PP
